  "narration": "One morning, an entire Louisiana lake disappeared down a hole in its own floor. It's November 1980 at Lake Peigneur. A crew drills for oil. Below, miners work a salt mine. First, the drill gets stuck. Then the platform shakes and tilts. The workers scramble off, and the lake starts spinning where their rig stood. The whirlpool grows, pulling in the drilling platform. Barges slide inward. Trees and pieces of shore follow. Meanwhile, underground, miners race for the exit as water floods the tunnels. They escape, but the lake above is still vanishing. The strangest part? Nobody knew this drain existed. Where does the water go? You see, beneath the lake is a network of salt-mine passages. Drilling appears to have opened a route into the mine. Water rushed in, and the ground collapsed, widening the opening. Imagine pulling a bathtub plug, except the bathtub is a lake and the drain is a mine. Then a coastal canal starts flowing backward. Water pours into the basin. The lake fills again, deeper and saltier. The drillers went looking for oil. The lake found a way to change its entire plumbing.",
